# Everything Vercel must not see.
#
# The website is the static files in web/ and nothing else. This repo also
# holds a Python desktop app, and Vercel's zero-config detection scans the
# deployment *source* -- not just the output directory -- so a main.py sitting
# next to a requirements.txt at the root is enough for it to decide this is a
# Python serverless function and fail looking for an `app` export.
#
# outputDirectory alone does not prevent that: it says where build output
# lands, not what gets detected. Excluding the Python entrypoints and manifests
# here means there is nothing left to detect, which is why this file is what
# actually fixes the deploy.
#
# Anchored patterns (leading /) matter: /models/ is the pickled classifier and
# must be excluded, while web/models/forest.json is what the browser demo
# loads and must ship.

# --- Python entrypoints: the direct cause of the failed detection ---
/main.py
/main_old.py
/ref.py
/conftest.py

# --- Python manifests: what makes Vercel reach for a Python runtime ---
/pyproject.toml
/requirements.txt
/requirements-*.txt
/setup.py
/setup.cfg

# --- Application and dev code ---
/gestureflow/
/scripts/
/tests/
/configs/

# --- Virtualenvs, caches, build artefacts ---
/venv/
/.venv/
/env/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
/.pytest_cache/
/.ruff_cache/
/.mypy_cache/

# --- Data and models. Note this is /models/ at the root, NOT web/models/ ---
/data/
/models/

# --- Local run output: benchmarks, recordings, reports ---
/bench/
/takes/
/reports/

# --- Repo furniture with no place on a CDN ---
/.github/
/*.md
/LICENSE
